messages.content:
In 'The Magic of the Mind', samādhi is discussed in this context: *"Whatever view such a one has, that becomes for him Right view;
whatever intention he has, that becomes for him Right Intention;
whatever effort he puts forth, that becomes for him Right Effort;
whatever mindfulness he has, that becomes for him Right
Mindfulness: and whatever concentration he has, that
becomes for him Right Concentration."* Here, samādhi (concentration (samādhi)) is treated not as a theoretical doctrine, but as a living faculty to be actively cultivated and observed. When we bring genuine mindfulness to our direct experience, the quality of samādhi helps dismantle habitual delusion and stabilizes the mind in clear, unentangled awareness.
